course: dictionary
lesson: 10194
title: Timing: the right moment
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Timing is the word for choosing the right moment to do something. «Her good timing meant she arrived just as the cake came out of the oven.» Good timing means something happened at exactly the right moment. Bad timing means it happened at the wrong moment.
explain: Timing can also mean measuring how long something takes. In science, a pupil may use a stopwatch to time how long ice takes to melt. The clock is the clue here: you are counting seconds, not choosing a moment.
explain: In music, sport and jokes, timing means doing things exactly on the beat. «Comedians need good timing» means a joke only works when the words land at the right moment. To work out which meaning fits, read the words around timing and ask: is this about a moment, or about how long something took?
fact: Timing is a noun, and it is also the -ing form of the verb «time».
fact: «Timing» can mean choosing the right moment, measuring how long something takes, or doing things exactly on the beat.
fact: «Good timing» means something happened at just the right moment; «bad timing» means it happened at the wrong moment.
fact: «GPS works because a phone listens for timing signals from several satellites» and works out where it is from how long the signals took.
fact: In everyday use, timing is usually uncountable, so we say «good timing», not «good timings».
exercise: Mia uses a stopwatch to check the timing of how long ice takes to melt. What is she measuring? | *How long something takes, counted in seconds on a clock | Which colour the ice is | How long something takes | How cold the room is
exercise: «Her timing was perfect: she arrived just as the cake came out of the oven.» What does perfect timing mean here? | A cake that was too big | *The right moment | The wrong moment | A very slow clock
exercise: Which phrase means something happened at the wrong moment? | good timing | perfect timing | *bad timing
test: «Good secrets are about timing, like a surprise party that ends in a happy moment.» In this sentence, timing means | Something that is very old | *Choosing the right moment | Measuring how long something takes | Playing the right note on a piano
test: A comedian's jokes land well because of timing. Timing here means | Writing very long jokes | Shouting loudly | A stopwatch | *Doing things exactly at the right moment
exam: In «A class of beginners sounds rough at first because everyone is learning breath, fingers and timing at once», what does timing mean? | Measuring how long a song lasts | Breathing in and out | Writing down the notes | *Playing together exactly on the beat
